## mps agent roles

- **A feature-sized request opens a vault artifact before it opens an editor.**
  Analysis → placement (which epic, which story) → an ADR and/or spec when the
  "how" is non-trivial → `mps-critic` → only then implementation →
  `mps-reviewer`. "Feature-sized" is not a judgement about how the request was
  phrased — it is about what the work touches: if you are about to write across
  several source files, open the story first.
- **Report instruction conflicts; do not arbitrate them.** If a session-level or
  harness-level instruction contradicts this block, say so and ask which wins.
  Resolving it silently is how the contradiction becomes invisible to the person
  who could have settled it.
- **Every role runs in a FRESH context, separate from the author.** How depends
  on the harness, the role does not: spawn the subagent where the harness has
  them (Claude Code, OpenCode), otherwise start a separate run with
  `mps agents show <role>` as its prompt (e.g. `codex exec "$(mps agents show critic) <target>"`).
  Reviewing your own work in your own context is the bias these roles exist to remove.
- After authoring or revising any vault artifact (ADR/spec/research/epic/story)
  or design proposal: run `mps-critic` on it before treating it final.
- Before implementing an epic/story: consult `mps-planner` — it plans against
  how prior epics map to the codebase (`code_refs`).
- After writing code, before commit / story-done: run `mps-reviewer` — it
  verifies the diff actually closes the story's acceptance criteria.
- When discussing vault contents, reference artifacts by their frontmatter
  `title:` (with their parent epic), never by session-invented shorthand.

